<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canadian–American Reciprocity Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Elgin–Marcy Treaty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Canadian–American Reciprocity Treaty, also called the Elgin–Marcy Treaty, was signed in 1854 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, on behalf of its North American colonies, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It removed duties on natural products such as grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and fish and opened fisheries and waterways to both sides. Trade grew strongly, but the United States ended the treaty in 1866, which encouraged the colonies towards Confederation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> repealed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corn-laws">Corn Laws</a> in 1846, ending imperial preference for colonial produce, the business community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> looked south, and merchants threatened to press for annexation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> unless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> secured a free trade agreement. The treaty took its other name from its negotiators, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-bruce-8th-earl-of-elgin">James Bruce, 8th Earl of Elgin</a>, and William L. Marcy. In the United States it was backed by manufacturers seeking cheaper raw materials and larger markets, and by free traders and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-united-states">Democratic Party</a> allies, while protected interests, represented by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republican-party-united-states">Republican Party</a>, opposed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty applied to British North America: the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, New Brunswick, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-edward-island">Prince Edward Island</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>. It admitted most Canadian raw materials and farm produce, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and wheat above all, to the American market free of duty, ending a 21% tariff on natural resources. In return Americans gained fishing rights off the east coast, and each side received some rights of navigation on the other's lakes and rivers. It was in force from 1854 to 1866.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Canadian exports to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> grew by 33% and American exports by 7%, and trade between the two countries doubled within ten years; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>'s coal mining gained in particular. In 1968 the economic historians Lawrence H. Officer and Lawrence B. Smith argued that railways, the American Civil War and the poor harvests and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> of 1855 explained most of the growth, and that open trade had replaced smuggling.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> ended the treaty in 1866, its critics objecting to the Cayley–Galt Tariff on manufactures and angry at British and colonial help to the Confederacy. Canada's later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-policy">National Policy</a> of tariffs began in 1879. In 1911 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid-laurier">Wilfrid Laurier</a>'s Liberals negotiated a new treaty of the same name with President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-howard-taft">William Howard Taft</a>, but the Conservatives warned of annexation and won that year's election, and Robert Borden's government rejected it. Free trade returned only with the Canada–United States Free Trade Agreement of 1988.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Canadian–American Reciprocity Treaty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
